        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-158412",
        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y,$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h,y)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=3{x}^{2}y$,  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x+h,y)=3(x+h)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{3(x+h)^{2}y-3{x}^{2}y}{h}).\n$$"
      },
