        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+2)^{3}},$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+2)^{3}},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+h+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}[\\frac{1}{h}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+h+2)^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(y+2)^{3}})].\n$$"
